component SigilClaimsVocabulary {
  goal {
    Own the names an interpreted claim may use, and the discipline for accepting
    returned text as data.
  }

  interface {
    AcceptedVocabulary {
      Publish the relation names a claim may use and the property names a
      property or measure row may use, each with its range. The set is closed: a
      claim, property or measure naming anything outside it is refused. A row
      carrying no such name is governed by the admission rules of the component
      that defines it. A generation records which accepted set a request was
      prepared against. It is declared rather than derived, and is bumped
      whenever the set changes so that an already-prepared request could no
      longer satisfy it.
    }

    AtomDiscipline {
      Returned text is accepted only as data-only egglog atoms: bytes bounded
      before parsing and atom count before validation, every rule, command,
      schedule and non-literal argument rejected, and the whole artifact refused
      rather than its valid portion.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    AcceptedVocabulary {
      The set is this component's own and is read from nothing while running. No
      change elsewhere in the compiler widens what an interpretation may return,
      and no component outside this one decides what the set contains. It always
      carries every name the closure's laws read; a set missing one is refused
      before any request is prepared, rather than leaving a law that never fires.
    }

    AtomDiscipline {
      Returned text is data and is never executed as a program. No file outside
      the compiled binary widens what is accepted.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    AcceptedVocabulary {
      The set began as a copy of the compiler's ontology and deliberately does
      not track it. Reading that ontology while running folded it into the
      interpretation fingerprint, so an edit anywhere in the compiler invalidated
      every prepared request; holding the two equal by test would have kept the
      same coupling in a weaker form. Being able to diverge is the point of
      owning the set, and it is what lets the compiler's own vocabulary be
      replaced without touching what an interpretation may return.
    }
  }

  cases {
    AcceptedVocabulary {
      A relation the compiler defines and this set omits is refused like any
      other unknown name.
    }
  }
}
